# A project's boards: `name  MAC  [hub  port]  [flags]  [serial=<usb serial>]
# [path=<by-path name>]  [# why]`.
#
# **Each project keeps its own**, at its root, listing only the boards that
# project works with. Copy this file there as `devices.list` (gitignored) and put
# your boards in:
#
#     cp garden/devices.list.example devices.list
#     $EDITOR devices.list
#
# Every tool in garden refuses a board that is not on the project's list: each
# project works with its own boards, and another project's are not its
# business. Garden itself has no list -- it shares the code, not the boards.
#
# Every script that can write to or power-cycle a device looks the board up here
# first, because /dev/ttyACM<n> numbering shuffles between plug-ins and a guard
# keyed to anything but the MAC is one replug away from protecting the wrong
# device.
#
# The MAC is on the by-id path the board enumerates as, and `tools/power.sh
# list` prints every board it can see:
#
#     ls /dev/serial/by-id/
#     usb-Espressif_USB_JTAG_serial_debug_unit_AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF-if00
#                                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
#
# A board with no MAC -- a small sensor flashed over a debug probe -- carries the
# id it derives from its die in the MAC's column, as the panel prints it.
#
# **Each identity once, and each name once.** Every lookup goes by the identity
# column (or the name) and takes the first entry that has it, so a second
# entry with the same MAC is never reached -- its `path=`, `serial=` and flags
# are silently ignored.
#
# `hub` and `port` are optional: `power.sh` finds them in sysfs while the board
# is plugged in, which is where the truth is. Recorded, they are what lets it
# cut a board that has stopped enumerating.
#
# ## serial
#
#   serial=<serial>   the USB serial of a console reached through a bridge with
#                     a serial of its own -- a CH343 -- which is not the board's
#                     MAC. It lets a port be traced to its board without opening
#                     it.
#
# ## path
#
#   path=<name>       for a board behind a bridge with no USB serial at all -- a
#                     CH340 -- the name of its socket under /dev/serial/by-path/,
#                     as `ls /dev/serial/by-path/` prints it (the whole path is
#                     read the same). Without it, such a port is refused by every
#                     check that asks which board is behind it, because sysfs
#                     cannot say.
#
# **A by-path name identifies the socket, not the board.** It is named after
# the USB port the bridge is plugged into, so whatever serial-less board sits in
# that socket is taken for this entry's board -- move the board, and the entry
# no longer finds it; plug another CH340 board in, and the entry finds that.
# What keeps a flash script built on `tools/board.sh` from writing to the wrong
# board, and what does not:
#
# * a device that has a serial of its own is always named by that serial, so a
#   board with one is never taken for a `path=` entry's board merely for sitting
#   in its socket -- it is taken for itself, which is right, but it may be
#   another of the project's boards than the one the image is for;
# * **so the flash script names the board its image is for**, and
#   `board_is` and `board_flash_guard` refuse the port when the board behind
#   it is any other;
# * **and it confirms a `path=` board before it writes**, from the MAC espflash
#   reads out of the chip on connect. `espflash board-info` prints it and
#   writes nothing; `board_flash_guard` refuses a `path=` board unless it is
#   given that output and the MAC is this entry's (`board_confirm_mac`).
#
# board-info opens the port, and opening it resets the chip, so the port is
# checked and the board's lock taken first, and every line's status checked:
#
#       socket="$(board_is "$PORT" "flashing the wrong board replaces its firmware" display)" || exit 1
#       lock="$(board_lock_path "$(board_of_port "$socket")")" || exit 1
#       exec 9>>"$lock" || exit 1
#       flock -n 9 || exit 1
#       info="$(espflash board-info --chip esp32 --port "$socket" 2>&1)" || exit 1
#       port="$(board_flash_guard "$socket" esp32 "$info" display)" || exit 1
#
# None of that holds for a script that leaves a step out. A console tool opens
# the port on the socket's word alone, and opening a CH340's port can restart
# the board behind it -- so keep a `path=` board in its own socket, and give
# the entry its `hub port` too.
#
# ## flags
#
#   forbidden   never write to it, never cut its power, wherever it is plugged.
#               For one of the project's own boards that must be left alone --
#               a probe to be replugged rather than cut, a live device whose
#               interruption costs more than reaching it. The text after `#` is
#               quoted back when a script refuses.
#
# ## What is refused
#
# `board.sh` refuses -- by MAC, wherever the board is plugged in -- a board that
# is not on this list, one flagged `forbidden`, and no board named at all; and a
# port whose board it cannot tell: no serial and no `path=` naming its socket, or
# a serial no entry claims. Every one of those fails closed, because the board
# that must not be touched is exactly the one a confused invocation would
# otherwise reach.
#
# `board_mac` with no name, as a project's flash script uses it, takes the first
# entry. `power.sh cycle` always needs a name.

# The entries below are commented out, so a list copied from this file holds no
# board until one is put in -- a placeholder left in, with a hub and port, could
# be cut at a real port that holds something else.
#
# name       MAC                  hub    port   flags       # why
# example    AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF    5-1    2
# bridged    11:22:33:44:55:66                serial=0123456789   # a console through a CH343
# display    AA:BB:CC:11:22:33    5-1    3      path=pci-0000:00:14.0-usb-0:1.3:1.0-port0   # a CH340: its socket
# probe      11:22:33:AA:BB:CC                forbidden           # replug to recover, never cut its power
